\cf0 The interesting feature is the 
\f1\b combination
\f0\b0  of 64 Machiavellianism + 68 psychopathy with 75 humility + 68 ethical leadership.\
That combination can produce a leader who is 
\f1\b decisive, politically astute, resilient and hard to intimidate
\f0\b0 , but whose leadership can become overly instrumental when stressed. Your strongest safeguards are that you appear to have relatively good empathy, high humility, and a fairly strong ethical orientation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Likely leadership style\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 You may naturally gravitate toward:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Strategic leadership.
\f0\b0  You probably think in terms of incentives, dependencies, likely reactions, and what will actually get an outcome rather than relying solely on formal process.\

\f1\b High decisiveness.
\f0\b0  You may be comfortable making uncomfortable calls, tolerating ambiguity, and acting when other people are still deliberating.\

\f1\b Composure under pressure.
\f0\b0  A higher psychopathy-related score can sometimes correspond to being less emotionally overwhelmed by situations that make other people anxious. In leadership, that can be useful during crises.\

\f1\b Low attachment to consensus for its own sake.
\f0\b0  You may be willing to disagree, challenge authority, cut through politics, or choose an unpopular course when you believe it is necessary.\

\f1\b Potentially transactional thinking.
\f0\b0  The downside is that people can start to look like variables in a system: useful, obstructive, influential, loyal, risky, etc. That can improve short-term execution while degrading trust if it becomes your dominant lens.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Likely biases to watch\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Your results suggest several 
\f2\i possible
\f0\i0  cognitive/leadership biases:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \'93Outcome justifies the method.\'94
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 When the objective feels important, you may be tempted to treat process, consultation, or relationship costs as secondary.\

\f1\b Strategic attribution.
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 You may interpret other people's behaviour through incentives and hidden agendas. Sometimes you're right; sometimes you're over-reading normal human behaviour as political manoeuvring.\

\f1\b Overconfidence in your emotional distance.
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 Being calm can be an advantage, but you may occasionally assume that because 
\f2\i you
\f0\i0  can tolerate something, others should be able to as well.\

\f1\b Speed bias.
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 You may prefer a fast, clear decision over a slower, messier process that gives people time to raise concerns.\

\f1\b Competence bias.
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 You may give disproportionate weight to people who are effective, tough, direct or strategically sophisticated, while undervaluing quieter contributors.\
Your 75 humility score is encouraging here: it gives you a good basis for deliberately correcting these tendencies.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

